2
00:00:40,318 --> 00:00:54,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Because we were speaking about it, in particular, with this, the word parimukhaṁ, and watching your mouth, which my mother used to tell me.

3
00:00:54,593 --> 00:00:55,514
[SPEAKER_00]: Watch your mouth, boy.

4
00:00:57,055 --> 00:01:00,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Funny, I knew what she meant.

5
00:01:07,499 --> 00:01:12,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I secluded from essential pleasures, secluded from unwholesome states.

6
00:01:13,904 --> 00:01:18,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It binds in the first jhāna, which is accompanied by thinking and pondering.

7
00:01:20,668 --> 00:01:29,654
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if one does not appropriate one's mouth, that area

8
00:01:34,051 --> 00:01:36,413
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what, you know, that's what you were saying the other day.

9
00:01:36,753 --> 00:01:37,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

10
00:01:39,374 --> 00:01:40,835
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that getting you to jhāna?

11
00:01:41,656 --> 00:01:42,316
[SPEAKER_01]: How does that help?

12
00:01:42,517 --> 00:01:43,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the...

13
00:01:44,118 --> 00:01:46,599
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we discussed it a few days ago as well.

14
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:57,046
[SPEAKER_00]: It was basically the... the presence of the mouth as an organ, you can say.

15
00:01:57,326 --> 00:01:59,908
[SPEAKER_00]: But basically, the presence of a mouth is a phenomenon.

16
00:02:00,855 --> 00:02:12,064
[SPEAKER_00]: In your experience, in your general experience, or daily experience, the presence of that mouth, basically, it's a... it's an entry point for the mind towards the body.

17
00:02:12,084 --> 00:02:14,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... it's a point of convergence, so to speak.

18
00:02:15,766 --> 00:02:27,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in your experience as a whole, the experience that has this physical body present, and all the possibility of the physical action, verbal action, mental action, eh... the domain of the body and the domain of the mind,

19
00:02:27,615 --> 00:02:43,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the entry point, so to speak, I mean, the mind doesn't really enter the body, but in terms of the relationship, when the mind is directed towards the body, it's the phenomenon, it's the phenomenon of the mouth, that's that first point.

20
00:02:44,271 --> 00:02:45,112
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have

21
00:02:46,807 --> 00:03:05,833
[SPEAKER_00]: If you have taken up your mouth, so to speak, if you assumed it to be yours, which implies all the possibilities based on that mouth, eye, speech, and anything that can be said, or any form of manipulation that can come out of that, if you have taken up the mouth, means you've taken up the whole domain of the body.

22
00:03:09,122 --> 00:03:10,183
[SPEAKER_01]: You say it's like a door.

23
00:03:10,743 --> 00:03:12,204
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a convergence point.

24
00:03:12,384 --> 00:03:13,925
[SPEAKER_00]: I suppose you can say the doorway.

25
00:03:13,985 --> 00:03:20,610
[SPEAKER_00]: You obviously need to be qualified, because it's not that you actually enter through that door.

26
00:03:20,730 --> 00:03:31,057
[SPEAKER_00]: But yes, it's the first point of reference, so to speak, in the relationship between the mind and the body.

27
00:03:32,598 --> 00:03:34,400
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if

28
00:03:35,514 --> 00:03:43,918
[SPEAKER_00]: You establish mindfulness in regard to the phenomenon of the mouth, in your experience.

29
00:03:44,478 --> 00:03:55,322
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, you're then guarded by that, or you're protected by anything that can come from the lower point, which is anything that's based on the bodily domain.

30
00:03:56,083 --> 00:04:01,405
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mind hasn't appropriated the point of convergence, means anything else that converge on it won't affect you.

31
00:04:03,408 --> 00:04:09,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically, yeah, like finding a gateway and then guarding that.

32
00:04:11,457 --> 00:04:15,481
[SPEAKER_00]: And the gateway towards the domain of the body is the phenomenon of the mouth.

33
00:04:17,323 --> 00:04:17,844
[SPEAKER_00]: Verbal.

34
00:04:19,466 --> 00:04:20,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Verbal involvement.

35
00:04:21,971 --> 00:04:37,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-verbal involvement, possibility of verbal involvement, all of them are basically, by giving in to them or by appropriating them, you are kind of... your mind is sort of descending towards the body, towards the bodily actions, towards anything that pertains to the body.

36
00:04:38,464 --> 00:04:49,973
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if the mind is established in regard to the phenomenon of the mouth, say, in the first jhāna, then basically you are secluded from anything bodily.

37
00:04:51,168 --> 00:04:55,190
[SPEAKER_00]: In a sense of anything that can arise on account of the five senses.

38
00:05:01,393 --> 00:05:02,674
[SPEAKER_01]: In what way do you guard?

39
00:05:02,694 --> 00:05:07,356
[SPEAKER_01]: How are you... how do you be mindful of the now?

40
00:05:07,437 --> 00:05:08,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

41
00:05:08,137 --> 00:05:09,878
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to recognize it as a phenomenon.

42
00:05:11,078 --> 00:05:19,663
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no more straightforward way of describing it, because it's a... it's a... it's a peripheral negative.

43
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:30,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the presence of the mouth is negative in regard to the act of speech, or any bodily act, or act of consumption.

44
00:05:33,416 --> 00:05:34,696
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to see it as such.

45
00:05:36,997 --> 00:05:41,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't actually have to go speaking in order to discern the verbal domain.

46
00:05:41,818 --> 00:05:50,380
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary, you actually don't speak in order for that negative to be discerned, negative of the mouth.

47
00:05:51,155 --> 00:05:58,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is basically, you can, just a different way of saying, the possibility of speech, the possibility of involvement, the possibility of verbal action.

48
00:05:59,719 --> 00:06:00,640
[SPEAKER_00]: All of them come from that.

49
00:06:01,721 --> 00:06:07,984
[SPEAKER_00]: But, it's all rooted down in a physical fact that you have mouth present, right there in your face.

50
00:06:10,365 --> 00:06:11,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be used.

51
00:06:11,946 --> 00:06:19,750
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, see, I mean, I'm uttering words, I'm saying things, I'm designating terms, but

52
00:06:20,727 --> 00:06:24,048
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all material, so to speak.

53
00:06:24,168 --> 00:06:29,990
[SPEAKER_00]: My mouth, the sounds you hear as well, as vibrations, frequencies, whatever, they respond to your ear.

54
00:06:30,610 --> 00:06:32,851
[SPEAKER_00]: It's all on the level of body and matter.

55
00:06:33,751 --> 00:06:39,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, it's basically, I'm kind of painting up my thoughts to you.

56
00:06:39,813 --> 00:06:40,833
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm designating them.

57
00:06:42,233 --> 00:06:51,204
[SPEAKER_00]: So you just naturally, by not being mindful in regard to that, you naturally assume that your thoughts just kind of flow into these words.

58
00:06:51,845 --> 00:06:54,948
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no... there is no clear point... well...

59
00:06:55,770 --> 00:07:01,035
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no point at all, when your thought suddenly changes into the word.

60
00:07:01,675 --> 00:07:04,518
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that which was a thought, enters the domain of the speech.

61
00:07:04,978 --> 00:07:05,158
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

62
00:07:05,719 --> 00:07:06,119
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't.

63
00:07:06,179 --> 00:07:08,181
[SPEAKER_00]: There isn't a single thread that connects that.

64
00:07:08,701 --> 00:07:12,244
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a domain of thoughts, then you have the domain of speech.

65
00:07:12,285 --> 00:07:14,687
[SPEAKER_00]: You have the domain of the body.

66
00:07:14,707 --> 00:07:21,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And they're completely independent of each other, and they can't mix, they can't connect, they can't interfere.

67
00:07:31,460 --> 00:07:32,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how do you speak then?

68
00:07:33,401 --> 00:07:36,964
[SPEAKER_00]: If there is nothing that comes from your thoughts, you speak by having thoughts.

69
00:07:37,804 --> 00:07:40,886
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, by breaking into the sound speech.

70
00:07:42,507 --> 00:07:42,988
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's it.

71
00:07:43,948 --> 00:07:45,129
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no smooth transition.

72
00:07:46,030 --> 00:07:56,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of thoughts has the possibility of it being simultaneously replicated, so to speak, in the domain of speech.

73
00:07:57,257 --> 00:07:59,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the verbal action, the power of speech.

74
00:08:10,931 --> 00:08:14,875
[SPEAKER_01]: How to purify your speech, you know, your wholesome speech?

75
00:08:15,615 --> 00:08:23,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if you're obviously, if you're mindful first, first, if you're mindful of unwholesome speech, so you don't do it, you restrain.

76
00:08:24,623 --> 00:08:37,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't necessarily see the purpose, you restrain from the unwholesome, unwholesome, saying unwholesome things, gossip, slander, malicious speech.

77
00:08:39,334 --> 00:08:40,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Greedy speech, anything like that.

78
00:08:41,315 --> 00:08:42,296
[SPEAKER_00]: You abstain from that.

79
00:08:47,138 --> 00:08:54,842
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, by not cultivating these... by not maintaining these unwholesome habits, habits that are rooted in greed, aversion, delusion...

80
00:08:55,623 --> 00:09:02,847
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, by having your speech restrained, then you can start to discern the negative, that negative in regard to the act of speech.

81
00:09:03,367 --> 00:09:05,288
[SPEAKER_00]: And the negative is just the presence of the mouth.

82
00:09:08,113 --> 00:09:16,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to me, it just seems important to, as you said, not to appropriate the mouth, because once you don't appropriate the mouth, then...

83
00:09:20,662 --> 00:09:22,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it kind of solves a lot of the issues.

84
00:09:23,344 --> 00:09:48,659
[SPEAKER_00]: You can, you can see, you can look at it basically, like, if you see this physical mouth that's kind of moving with the tongue and teeth and so on, if you see that as that matter because of which you're able to speak, the thoughts are able to be replicated in the domain of speech because of that physical mouth, by remaining mindful of the physical mouth, you won't be able to appropriate, you won't be confusing the two.

85
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:49,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Because why?

86
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:55,202
[SPEAKER_00]: You see its necessary cause, necessary determination, which is presence of the physical mouth.

87
00:09:56,663 --> 00:10:04,507
[SPEAKER_00]: As negative as that mouth is, in your experience, when you engage in speech, it's still a real thing, and it is a physical mouth, presence of the physical mouth.

88
00:10:04,567 --> 00:10:09,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Without it, it would just be simply inconceivable to speak.

89
00:10:14,351 --> 00:10:19,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously, you don't know how to speak immediately.

90
00:10:22,232 --> 00:10:31,179
[SPEAKER_00]: And that just shows you the inherent separation between these domains, the verbal domain and the mental domain.

91
00:10:31,499 --> 00:10:33,241
[SPEAKER_00]: Baby can't speak.

92
00:10:33,361 --> 00:10:34,342
[SPEAKER_00]: Takes quite some time.

93
00:10:35,202 --> 00:10:39,906
[SPEAKER_00]: You start making sound, then you start hearing the sounds that you yourself are making.

94
00:10:40,807 --> 00:10:42,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, obviously, you hear the sounds that others are making.

95
00:10:43,729 --> 00:10:51,315
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, basically, you start fabricating the connections between the thoughts, words and verbal expressions.

96
00:10:52,782 --> 00:10:56,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, obviously, the whole assumption grows in regard to it, where you just start assuming the same.

97
00:10:57,064 --> 00:10:58,824
[SPEAKER_00]: You know.

98
00:10:59,024 --> 00:11:00,085
[SPEAKER_00]: I speak what I think.

99
00:11:00,745 --> 00:11:09,828
[SPEAKER_00]: What I think is what I speak.

100
00:11:09,848 --> 00:11:10,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So it needs to be.

101
00:11:10,649 --> 00:11:11,329
[SPEAKER_00]: That needs to happen.

102
00:11:11,709 --> 00:11:18,332
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have that proliferation in order to be able to discern it and then not appropriate it.

103
00:11:24,896 --> 00:11:30,381
[SPEAKER_00]: But in terms of the meditation, yeah, you can just be mindful of the presence of the mouth.

104
00:11:31,121 --> 00:11:31,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing in particular.

105
00:11:32,002 --> 00:11:36,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Don't observe your tongue or whatever, roof, top of your mouth, or something on your lips.

106
00:11:36,505 --> 00:11:37,106
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ridiculous.

107
00:11:37,806 --> 00:11:39,368
[SPEAKER_00]: Just the fact that you have mouth present.

108
00:11:40,649 --> 00:11:46,233
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can basically be mindful of, like, of the front of your face, so to speak.

109
00:11:47,394 --> 00:11:51,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the whole head, if you want, but basically the front, usually.

110
00:11:53,253 --> 00:12:19,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're mindful of your mouth, and when that mouth speaks, when that mouth is making sounds, or the possibility that that mouth might be making sounds, well, you start putting like a buffer between you speaking, because you might want to speak, but it's your mouth that speaks.

111
00:12:21,913 --> 00:12:22,914
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly speak.

112
00:12:25,555 --> 00:12:29,398
[SPEAKER_00]: If you could speak, you wouldn't need mouth to speak.

113
00:12:29,418 --> 00:12:31,199
[SPEAKER_00]: But the speech is inseparable from the mouth.

114
00:12:31,779 --> 00:12:36,102
[SPEAKER_01]: It's difficult when you're not speaking, and you're mindful of the mouth.

115
00:12:37,303 --> 00:12:38,304
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of easier.

116
00:12:38,384 --> 00:12:42,006
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you start speaking, it's... You immediately lose the mindfulness, and that's the whole point.

117
00:12:42,827 --> 00:12:45,929
[SPEAKER_00]: You train that mindfulness, or rather you refine it to the point

118
00:12:47,104 --> 00:12:57,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That it ascends in the general background to such a level whereby even when you speak, that mindfulness doesn't get disturbed.

119
00:12:57,358 --> 00:13:03,506
[SPEAKER_00]: And by not having that mindfulness disturbed, there won't be any room for introducing the assumption of I am speaking.

120
00:13:22,703 --> 00:13:27,886
[SPEAKER_01]: And mouth, as a physical organ, has lots of other, obviously, significance.

121
00:13:28,826 --> 00:13:35,249
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as we spoke before, you know, it is a point of consumption.

122
00:13:35,329 --> 00:13:36,370
[SPEAKER_00]: You consume things.

123
00:13:37,050 --> 00:13:46,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because the self, it's most closely, and probably, like, well, probably right off the bat, basically, as soon as you're born, baby starts identifying.

124
00:13:47,708 --> 00:13:50,830
[SPEAKER_00]: It's sense of self, underlying tendencies towards the sense of self with the mouth.

125
00:13:51,570 --> 00:14:01,915
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what children... you're putting things in your mouth, everything, like objects, anything, because that is a way of assimilating them, of appropriating them, consuming them, or just of getting to know them.

126
00:14:07,078 --> 00:14:07,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Chew it.

127
00:14:07,919 --> 00:14:08,219
[SPEAKER_00]: Get it.

128
00:14:08,659 --> 00:14:12,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Feels like you are touching it with your sense of self.

129
00:14:13,453 --> 00:14:22,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, obviously, that kind of grows further, and then you have various expressions that develop on top of that.

130
00:14:23,298 --> 00:14:24,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the speech and all that.

131
00:14:24,719 --> 00:14:30,202
[SPEAKER_00]: But basically, always, the food will always remain connected to the mouth, connected to eating.

132
00:14:30,542 --> 00:14:32,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And even sensuality in general.

133
00:14:33,984 --> 00:14:36,325
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... the mouth plays quite a big significance.

134
00:14:41,902 --> 00:14:52,610
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it is a... it is a... it is a... almost like a symbol of assimilation, or rather stands for the assumption of assimilation and conception, or, simply said, appropriation.

135
00:14:53,731 --> 00:15:02,618
[SPEAKER_00]: Because when you eat the food, the food doesn't change in the sense of... it doesn't really become... it doesn't disappear from the world, so to speak.

136
00:15:03,138 --> 00:15:04,099
[SPEAKER_00]: It remains external.

137
00:15:04,779 --> 00:15:08,002
[SPEAKER_00]: The food that was on the table, it's now mashed up in your stomach.

138
00:15:09,141 --> 00:15:12,443
[SPEAKER_00]: But that stomach and the table, they're all part of the external world.

139
00:15:13,443 --> 00:15:17,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, consuming the food is just having a revolve around these external things.

140
00:15:18,425 --> 00:15:21,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't actually get inside, in me.

141
00:15:22,007 --> 00:15:24,368
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't consume it, because I am not in the stomach.

142
00:15:25,088 --> 00:15:29,891
[SPEAKER_00]: The stomach is external, same as mouth is external, same as the table is external.

143
00:15:33,027 --> 00:15:39,288
[SPEAKER_00]: But the sense of self assumes the ownership, so it just feels as if it is on the other side, on the inside of the self, so to speak.

144
00:15:39,989 --> 00:15:45,089
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the food is always that kind of subtle maintenance of that assumption.

145
00:15:46,450 --> 00:15:54,331
[SPEAKER_00]: By eating unmindfully, you are basically maintaining the assumption of the self is consuming the food that's now disappearing behind it.

146
00:15:54,711 --> 00:15:55,851
[SPEAKER_00]: It's being taken.

147
00:15:56,972 --> 00:15:57,432
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't.

148
00:15:57,572 --> 00:15:59,332
[SPEAKER_00]: It stays in that external world.

149
00:16:00,495 --> 00:16:08,197
[SPEAKER_00]: Just goes through the mouth, contacts you with a taste, and then stays there, basically.

150
00:16:08,397 --> 00:16:09,418
[SPEAKER_00]: Stays in the external world.

151
00:16:09,538 --> 00:16:09,958
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the thing.

152
00:16:09,978 --> 00:16:12,679
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be moderate in your eating.

153
00:16:13,799 --> 00:16:13,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

154
00:16:14,039 --> 00:16:16,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you can choose, I'll have a little bit of this, a little bit of that.

155
00:16:16,900 --> 00:16:17,580
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you...

156
00:16:17,900 --> 00:16:20,121
[SPEAKER_01]: But completely, it's a complete blur.

157
00:16:20,761 --> 00:16:21,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

158
00:16:21,882 --> 00:16:26,023
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll have this, and then I'll ring a bell, and then I'll bow, and then I'll eat my

159
00:16:26,442 --> 00:16:28,463
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll carry now and then I'll bow.

160
00:16:29,904 --> 00:16:33,946
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's just like a random observance, basically.

161
00:16:34,026 --> 00:16:35,127
[SPEAKER_00]: That means nothing.

162
00:16:36,507 --> 00:16:42,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, you know, if you're pigging out and eating carelessly, well, there's no practice there.

163
00:16:42,571 --> 00:16:46,693
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, either way, you can abstain from food entirely and still not develop any wisdom.

164
00:16:47,033 --> 00:16:52,256
[SPEAKER_01]: The only way that you're supposed to eat the Buddha's chest is mindfully.

165
00:16:52,716 --> 00:16:53,857
[SPEAKER_01]: And, well,

166
00:16:56,762 --> 00:17:01,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness, basically, already implies a degree of discernment and knowledge.

167
00:17:02,164 --> 00:17:06,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness has nothing to do with the ritual you might observe before your meal, and during your meal, and after your meal.

168
00:17:07,446 --> 00:17:08,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Be present with the taste.

169
00:17:08,946 --> 00:17:09,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Be present with the taste.

170
00:17:09,947 --> 00:17:10,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

171
00:17:10,667 --> 00:17:14,588
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I mean, if you want to do that, sure, but that might not necessarily lead you to anything.

172
00:17:14,648 --> 00:17:16,669
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the taste is present one way or the other.

173
00:17:17,893 --> 00:17:20,734
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't... and that's the point that you need to recognize, which you don't.

174
00:17:21,094 --> 00:17:29,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I must be present with these tastes, whereby you imply that if you're not present with the taste, what, the taste is gone, or the taste hasn't arisen, or the taste... No, the taste is there.

175
00:17:31,496 --> 00:17:33,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means you have no choice but to be present with the taste.

176
00:17:34,556 --> 00:17:38,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why making the extra effort on top of it, or being present with the taste, when you are present with the taste?

177
00:17:40,537 --> 00:17:44,558
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is... that is, again, the taste, or rather the senses in themselves,

178
00:17:45,838 --> 00:17:54,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Are also, in themselves, a point... well, it's basically a point where people get deceived.

179
00:17:54,825 --> 00:18:11,077
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you... like in this particular example, when you... when a person thinks, or rather a person assumes, like, eating the food and the disappearance of the food through the mouth and chewing, stands for the assimilation of that food, stands for consumption, disappearance.

180
00:18:11,417 --> 00:18:12,258
[SPEAKER_00]: The self took it.

181
00:18:13,792 --> 00:18:18,893
[SPEAKER_00]: But basically, that food has just moved from one external point to another external point.

182
00:18:19,013 --> 00:18:22,614
[SPEAKER_00]: From the table into the stomach, down the toilet drain, and so on.

183
00:18:23,074 --> 00:18:24,395
[SPEAKER_00]: Part of it staying in the body.

184
00:18:24,855 --> 00:18:28,896
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that body, that table, that stomach, all of that remains external.

185
00:18:29,996 --> 00:18:37,758
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is absolutely no point from that external, from which this food can arise and get close to the sense of self.

186
00:18:39,677 --> 00:18:44,161
[SPEAKER_00]: What does get close to the sense of self is the taste, contacted by taste.

187
00:18:45,683 --> 00:18:46,463
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

188
00:18:46,904 --> 00:18:49,906
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that confusion sets.

189
00:18:49,946 --> 00:18:51,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes with the sight, with the ear.

190
00:18:52,709 --> 00:18:57,653
[SPEAKER_00]: Because your eyes are objects in the world.

191
00:18:57,714 --> 00:18:59,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Objects you see in the world are objects in the world.

192
00:19:00,816 --> 00:19:04,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But the vision in itself, it's not quite, isn't it?

193
00:19:06,795 --> 00:19:12,217
[SPEAKER_00]: You see the world, but the vision in itself, that which you can't see, eh... hmm... where is that then?

194
00:19:13,637 --> 00:19:18,319
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you just assume that to be where yourself is, so to speak, because it's neither here nor there.

195
00:19:19,519 --> 00:19:26,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And by taking that up, then you create this division between the external, the sense objects, and the internal, me.

196
00:19:28,262 --> 00:19:31,163
[SPEAKER_00]: By taking up the vision... Well, basically, you appropriate the sense bases.

197
00:19:31,343 --> 00:19:36,185
[SPEAKER_00]: You end up appropriating your body by not having your mind developed in regard to the

198
00:19:37,556 --> 00:19:38,017
[SPEAKER_00]: Contact.

199
00:19:38,977 --> 00:19:43,180
[SPEAKER_00]: Contact by eyes, contact by sight, contact by taste, contact by sound.

200
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:43,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Vision.

201
00:19:44,461 --> 00:19:44,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Or vision.

202
00:19:45,101 --> 00:19:45,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

203
00:19:45,781 --> 00:19:46,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Taste.

204
00:19:46,762 --> 00:19:47,002
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

205
00:19:51,465 --> 00:20:01,211
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you were to see that, be it an external object, be it an internal sense base, such as your tongue or your eye, eye organ,

206
00:20:02,370 --> 00:20:05,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Would... like, okay, let's go back to the taste.

207
00:20:06,012 --> 00:20:19,421
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there's no tongue, if there's no mouth, and if there is nothing to be consumed, eaten, put in the mouth, would a taste be possible to arise?

208
00:20:21,503 --> 00:20:22,303
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

209
00:20:23,524 --> 00:20:31,089
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to... and like, not just that it wouldn't be impossible to arise, it would be also impossible to remain standing if these things disappear.

210
00:20:33,856 --> 00:20:42,582
[SPEAKER_00]: You might have an arisen taste, but it's suddenly... well, it's suddenly you lose your sense of... sense of taste, you lose your tongue, you lose your head.

211
00:20:43,122 --> 00:20:44,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Can this taste remain?

212
00:20:44,303 --> 00:20:45,164
[SPEAKER_00]: It can't.

213
00:20:45,184 --> 00:20:55,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, no matter how closely tastes, or vision, or sounds pressure you, they also remain external, because they are rooted in external, and they can't break away from it.

214
00:20:56,091 --> 00:20:56,792
[SPEAKER_00]: It's inconceivable.

215
00:20:57,357 --> 00:21:00,760
[SPEAKER_01]: But once you see that the internal sense plays as... As external.

216
00:21:01,221 --> 00:21:01,801
[SPEAKER_01]: As external.

217
00:21:02,201 --> 00:21:04,984
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the external object, as even more external.

218
00:21:05,444 --> 00:21:05,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

219
00:21:09,008 --> 00:21:23,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you see the, the eye, or the internal senses as external, in the body, belonging to the body over there, then,

220
00:21:27,954 --> 00:21:31,357
[SPEAKER_01]: That middle part, the vision, the sounds...

221
00:21:36,341 --> 00:21:38,363
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... where... you said, where is it?

222
00:21:38,603 --> 00:21:40,204
[SPEAKER_01]: But I think it's in between.

223
00:21:40,685 --> 00:21:42,266
[SPEAKER_00]: It is in between, and it stays in between.

224
00:21:42,866 --> 00:21:51,573
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, when you're appropriately in between, then... well, then you make it liable to being underlined, underlied.

225
00:21:52,355 --> 00:21:57,579
[SPEAKER_00]: By the... by the tendency of the eye, the tendencies of the nose, the tendencies of the tongue.

226
00:21:57,599 --> 00:22:00,581
[SPEAKER_01]: But as I just said, one said, eye is not appropriated.

227
00:22:01,042 --> 00:22:02,202
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't appropriate eye...

228
00:22:03,343 --> 00:22:05,365
[SPEAKER_01]: If your mindfulness is properly...

229
00:22:05,445 --> 00:22:12,630
[SPEAKER_00]: If your mindfulness is properly established in regard to the... the eye organs that are present in the world, is that because of which the sight is there in the first place?

230
00:22:12,650 --> 00:22:12,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

231
00:22:13,591 --> 00:22:18,235
[SPEAKER_00]: If that's established, then exactly, there is nothing underlining... underlying that whole picture.

232
00:22:19,096 --> 00:22:21,538
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything is now pushed externally, i.e.

233
00:22:22,019 --> 00:22:26,322
[SPEAKER_00]: everything is now not taken to be mine, not taken to be pertaining to me.

234
00:22:26,903 --> 00:22:28,344
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's nothing underlying it.

235
00:22:28,905 --> 00:22:29,785
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing underneath.

236
00:22:30,826 --> 00:22:33,148
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to be... That you otherwise assume.

237
00:22:33,249 --> 00:22:33,989
[SPEAKER_00]: You always assume.

238
00:22:34,570 --> 00:22:43,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, when you're eating, basically, your tongue, your sense of taste, your sense base of taste, that is assumed as underneath.

239
00:22:44,699 --> 00:22:45,760
[SPEAKER_00]: That is assumed as mine.

240
00:22:46,912 --> 00:22:51,253
[SPEAKER_00]: And the consumption of the food, the disappearance of the food, is disappearing under that niche.

241
00:22:52,913 --> 00:22:54,013
[SPEAKER_01]: But isn't it enough?

242
00:22:54,714 --> 00:22:57,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's a further investigation.

243
00:22:58,094 --> 00:23:05,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you think it's internal senses, then you're investigating the external form and so on.

244
00:23:06,576 --> 00:23:11,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense... sense objects.

245
00:23:12,217 --> 00:23:15,098
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you're investigating with vision and sound and taste.

246
00:23:15,258 --> 00:23:15,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

247
00:23:16,399 --> 00:23:18,300
[SPEAKER_01]: But, do you have to go that far?

248
00:23:18,860 --> 00:23:23,041
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I wasn't talking about investigation of it, because that already implies quite a lot of wrong views.

249
00:23:23,701 --> 00:23:28,923
[SPEAKER_00]: People investigating end up explaining it to themselves, how these things work.

250
00:23:29,143 --> 00:23:31,183
[SPEAKER_00]: That's irrelevant.

251
00:23:31,203 --> 00:23:32,044
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

252
00:23:32,064 --> 00:23:32,724
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe just...

253
00:23:32,744 --> 00:23:34,184
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm not talking about investigation.

254
00:23:34,204 --> 00:23:43,187
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm talking about the direct discernment of the... and I think that's what you're aiming at as well, the direct discernment of the sense base as that which is already in the world.

255
00:23:44,212 --> 00:23:48,296
[SPEAKER_00]: And by being that which is already in the world like a sense organ, you can't really control it.

256
00:23:48,836 --> 00:23:49,557
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no say.

257
00:23:50,258 --> 00:23:56,964
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, your eyes are there working, quite an intricate organs, with all the nerves and optic nerves and so on.

258
00:23:57,164 --> 00:24:00,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have absolutely, absolutely no access to it.

259
00:24:02,089 --> 00:24:03,030
[SPEAKER_00]: And you have no say.

260
00:24:04,189 --> 00:24:04,429
[SPEAKER_00]: In it.

261
00:24:04,730 --> 00:24:09,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if that thing fails, for whatever random reason, your sight is gone.

262
00:24:10,075 --> 00:24:24,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Your whole world of vision, everything you've ever seen, everything you ever would see, everything you're seeing right now, everything that you ever felt on account of what you've seen, everything that you ever done, that you would do on account of what you've seen, is gone.

263
00:24:26,269 --> 00:24:31,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Because none of that would be able to remain standing if these fleshy eye organs are not there.

264
00:24:32,815 --> 00:24:41,602
[SPEAKER_00]: So, recognize, oh, I can't possibly own this, even if I were to, you know, to make the extra effort to do so.

265
00:24:41,682 --> 00:24:42,103
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't.

266
00:24:43,482 --> 00:24:52,064
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say... and if you start seeing that, then you can even say, oh, these are my eyes, but it won't actually stand for the assumption of my mind.

267
00:24:52,744 --> 00:24:53,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's obvious.

268
00:24:53,784 --> 00:24:54,584
[SPEAKER_00]: It just becomes obvious.

269
00:24:55,004 --> 00:24:58,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not that you have to see it and then stop owning it.

270
00:24:58,505 --> 00:25:02,806
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, seeing directly automatically results in disowning.

271
00:25:03,506 --> 00:25:07,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Disowning everything else that... stands upon it, which is everything else, basically.

272
00:25:08,107 --> 00:25:08,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

273
00:25:09,222 --> 00:25:10,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I'm talking about.

274
00:25:10,863 --> 00:25:13,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in... Like, I don't have to look at the things that stand upon.

275
00:25:13,344 --> 00:25:14,285
[SPEAKER_00]: No, of course not.

276
00:25:14,305 --> 00:25:14,725
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

277
00:25:14,745 --> 00:25:15,646
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a complete mistake.

278
00:25:15,706 --> 00:25:22,850
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically the science, the worldly science, of looking at the things, or psychology, of explaining things and... That's what I mean.

279
00:25:22,870 --> 00:25:24,471
[SPEAKER_00]: ...drawing parallel connections, etc.

280
00:25:24,491 --> 00:25:32,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to do with... By staying mindful of that sense base, the body, that body there, properly, that's enough.

281
00:25:32,695 --> 00:25:37,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's enough for you, because you've heard enough, in terms of what you're supposed to see.

282
00:25:38,747 --> 00:25:47,734
[SPEAKER_00]: But if somebody just comes to it, with all the wrong views that they still have, and then you say to them, just be mindful of that, it won't mean anything, and certainly won't result in anything.

283
00:25:48,134 --> 00:25:51,657
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, you can just be mindful of that, when you know how to regard it.

284
00:25:52,017 --> 00:25:55,180
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as often they talk in the suttas, so, how should I regard the I?

285
00:25:55,340 --> 00:25:58,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, regard it as not mine, not me, not I am.

286
00:26:00,684 --> 00:26:04,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, yeah, yeah, be mindful of that, because that attitude is already implied,

287
00:26:05,548 --> 00:26:13,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Through practice, through training, that attitude, you basically... you suffuse your mindfulness with that attitude.

288
00:26:15,538 --> 00:26:19,361
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can only do so when you know what the attitude is that needs to be developed.

289
00:26:20,041 --> 00:26:22,723
[SPEAKER_00]: The attitude of disowning, disappropriation, dispassion.

290
00:26:22,843 --> 00:26:23,684
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not a thought.

291
00:26:24,205 --> 00:26:24,325
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

292
00:26:24,345 --> 00:26:25,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, this is not mine.

293
00:26:25,385 --> 00:26:25,826
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

294
00:26:25,866 --> 00:26:27,827
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already too coarse, that thought.

295
00:26:28,007 --> 00:26:30,349
[SPEAKER_00]: It's already, it's already too, too coarse.

296
00:26:30,449 --> 00:26:38,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you're trying to, you know, to pull a, pull a thread through the, through the eye of the needle, well, if the thread's too big, it just won't fit.

297
00:26:39,636 --> 00:26:42,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to refine it, refine it until it can go through.

298
00:26:44,043 --> 00:26:50,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sure, you can say attitude is a form of thought, but it's a much subtler way of thought than actual course thinking.

299
00:26:54,893 --> 00:26:58,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Because saying, yeah, it's not your... Yeah, it doesn't mean anything.

300
00:26:58,657 --> 00:27:05,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, watching that...

301
00:27:07,593 --> 00:27:07,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Space.

302
00:27:09,174 --> 00:27:11,756
[SPEAKER_01]: The space is a face.

303
00:27:12,477 --> 00:27:13,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Your mouth, that space.

304
00:27:15,159 --> 00:27:19,603
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that can bring you closer to seeing your sense objects.

305
00:27:21,131 --> 00:27:32,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry, your sense bases, such as eyes, nose, ears, tongue, well, even face of touch, basically, the head is the foremost, you know, the head is your identity, the face.

306
00:27:32,317 --> 00:27:40,401
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having mindfulness established to the fore of that face, in regard to that face, yeah, you're having a mindfulness established in regard to the sense bases.

307
00:27:44,623 --> 00:27:50,586
[SPEAKER_01]: And being... and not appropriating, or should I say, being mindful of that

308
00:27:52,178 --> 00:28:04,821
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense bases secludes me from... Well, secludes you from anything that is... that those sense bases are subjected to.

309
00:28:05,321 --> 00:28:05,741
[SPEAKER_00]: Anything.

310
00:28:06,321 --> 00:28:06,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

311
00:28:07,342 --> 00:28:08,242
[SPEAKER_01]: That they are subjected to.

312
00:28:12,443 --> 00:28:19,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I hear a loud sound or see something intense sight.

313
00:28:19,565 --> 00:28:20,285
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because of my

314
00:28:21,381 --> 00:28:23,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness has been developed strong enough.

315
00:28:23,242 --> 00:28:32,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You'd see that that sight or sound pertains to the actual sense organ that is out there with that sight and sound, not out of you, because you stopped assuming that now.

316
00:28:33,267 --> 00:28:34,108
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the thing.

317
00:28:34,148 --> 00:28:36,929
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... you've stopped assuming the body.

318
00:28:38,590 --> 00:28:40,371
[SPEAKER_01]: You see it where it is, out there.

319
00:28:42,312 --> 00:28:49,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't... you can't be inside it, but you are not exactly anywhere, in particular, either.

320
00:28:51,058 --> 00:28:51,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

321
00:28:53,279 --> 00:28:59,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, those visions, those tastes, can't go into you?

322
00:29:00,402 --> 00:29:07,225
[SPEAKER_00]: No, because you is not rooted anymore to that which those visions and sounds pertain to.

323
00:29:09,406 --> 00:29:19,330
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through the appropriation of the sense bases, or through being... through not having mindfulness developed to the extent necessary in regard to the sense bases, that which those sense bases are subjected to,

324
00:29:20,528 --> 00:29:23,751
[SPEAKER_00]: You are subjected to it.

325
00:29:23,791 --> 00:29:25,913
[SPEAKER_00]: These sounds enter you.

326
00:29:25,933 --> 00:29:27,235
[SPEAKER_00]: This vision harasses you.

327
00:29:27,935 --> 00:29:29,437
[SPEAKER_00]: It incites you.

328
00:29:30,238 --> 00:29:32,100
[SPEAKER_00]: The taste arises in you.

329
00:29:32,120 --> 00:29:33,921
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's all me in regard to me.

330
00:29:34,922 --> 00:29:43,451
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you become established in regard to this sense organs, as the organs, as external organs, which is the only way you can be established in regard to them,

331
00:29:44,150 --> 00:29:48,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That internal dimension, that internal assumption of them, will fade.

332
00:29:48,131 --> 00:29:48,351
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

333
00:29:48,391 --> 00:29:56,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Because with the proper mindfulness established, it becomes apparent that the sights pertain to the eyes, the sounds pertain to the ears.

334
00:29:56,153 --> 00:29:58,373
[SPEAKER_01]: So that keeps working the way they've always done.

335
00:29:58,613 --> 00:29:59,213
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

336
00:29:59,673 --> 00:29:59,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

337
00:30:00,254 --> 00:30:03,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And you recognize there was never even room for your sense of self in it.

338
00:30:03,574 --> 00:30:06,535
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just not knowing that there was no room, you assumed it there.

339
00:30:07,468 --> 00:30:09,989
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why in the scene, there will only be the scene.

340
00:30:10,009 --> 00:30:11,890
[SPEAKER_01]: In the herd, there'll only be the herd.

341
00:30:11,950 --> 00:30:14,472
[SPEAKER_01]: It just carries on, yeah.

342
00:30:14,512 --> 00:30:15,352
[SPEAKER_00]: For scene, there's scene.

343
00:30:15,532 --> 00:30:16,473
[SPEAKER_00]: In herd, there's herd.

344
00:30:16,553 --> 00:30:17,373
[SPEAKER_00]: In taste, there's taste.

345
00:30:17,733 --> 00:30:18,414
[SPEAKER_00]: There's nothing else.

346
00:30:18,814 --> 00:30:24,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's... there's this, maybe, thought that you won't see anything anymore.

347
00:30:25,477 --> 00:30:26,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, maybe.

348
00:30:26,698 --> 00:30:28,439
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's not... not the case.

349
00:30:30,680 --> 00:30:33,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there anything further away from you all?

350
00:30:35,422 --> 00:30:36,563
[SPEAKER_01]: In that convergence point?

351
00:30:38,580 --> 00:30:43,763
[SPEAKER_01]: From your mouth, it's, you know, anything's further away, even down your body.

352
00:30:44,103 --> 00:30:47,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just a, just a bigger step away, even further away.

353
00:30:47,785 --> 00:31:00,751
[SPEAKER_00]: It's exactly, yeah, the convergence of the mouth, the face, it's, it's basically the way the body is structured, you know, if your eyes were on top of your palms or something, if things wouldn't work the same way, but they're not.

354
00:31:01,717 --> 00:31:05,299
[SPEAKER_00]: All of the major sense organs are based in your head.

355
00:31:06,119 --> 00:31:07,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus the head, thus the face.

356
00:31:09,581 --> 00:31:09,982
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

357
00:31:10,042 --> 00:31:11,362
[SPEAKER_00]: They're all based on the front of your face.

358
00:31:12,183 --> 00:31:14,504
[SPEAKER_00]: And ears to the side, but still close to the front.

359
00:31:16,025 --> 00:31:24,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you're mindful of that physical presence, as that because of which the vision in itself

360
00:31:26,038 --> 00:31:34,306
[SPEAKER_00]: The sounds, the sphere of sounds, the smells, tastes, touches, is there.

361
00:31:36,988 --> 00:31:41,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, basically, you cease to be liable to it, or rather, you're not being affected by it.

362
00:31:46,085 --> 00:31:47,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Can anyone be mindful of that?

363
00:31:49,987 --> 00:31:51,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Not anyone can just be mindful of that.

364
00:31:51,687 --> 00:31:59,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no, because mindfulness, it's not just pure awareness, which such thing doesn't exist.

365
00:32:00,211 --> 00:32:01,151
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not pure awareness.

366
00:32:01,451 --> 00:32:03,472
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not aware of anything.

367
00:32:03,492 --> 00:32:15,657
[SPEAKER_00]: The mindfulness, the right mindfulness, is basically inseparable from discernment, that can then lead to the proper knowledge of the nature of things.

368
00:32:17,523 --> 00:32:20,366
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to... you can't just be mindful.

369
00:32:21,807 --> 00:32:22,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Of your face?

370
00:32:22,408 --> 00:32:25,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because that... yeah, be mindful of my face and then what?

371
00:32:26,472 --> 00:32:26,592
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

372
00:32:26,832 --> 00:32:28,373
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to be mindful in the right way.

373
00:32:28,413 --> 00:32:29,434
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to discern it.

374
00:32:30,035 --> 00:32:33,298
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can be mindful if your face doesn't mean anything.

375
00:32:33,838 --> 00:32:42,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you're mindful of your face, as that because of which the entire world that's right simultaneously present there, is standing right there,

376
00:32:43,855 --> 00:32:52,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you're mindful, basically, of the relationship between the two, of the determination and that which is being determined.

377
00:32:52,577 --> 00:32:59,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And that determination, it's basically that face, presence of these sense organs, operating sense organs.

378
00:33:00,759 --> 00:33:09,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And, see, there is a whole world that simultaneously stands upon that, including your sense of self, upon the presence of these sense organs, and you can't even

379
00:33:11,648 --> 00:33:27,863
[SPEAKER_00]: On the level of your abstraction, you can't even conceive how it would be if you were to be in control of these seven sorghums, to control the workings, inner workings of the of the eye, of the lump of flesh that is your eye.

380
00:33:28,644 --> 00:33:29,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Inconceivable.

381
00:33:29,625 --> 00:33:36,732
[SPEAKER_00]: You can observe it in the others, but they will never be your working eyes, because that's what you see with your working eye.

382
00:33:37,798 --> 00:33:39,959
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you can infer, that's my working eye.

383
00:33:40,599 --> 00:33:54,025
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if that inference, so to speak, does not stand as determination for the world that's simultaneously present as you're speaking it, as you're seeing it, then it will not be seen correctly.

384
00:33:54,985 --> 00:34:00,487
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, eh... it looks the same, externally.

385
00:34:01,168 --> 00:34:06,370
[SPEAKER_00]: The description that the Buddha gave of the senses, and the description that the modern medical science tells us.

386
00:34:07,295 --> 00:34:08,975
[SPEAKER_00]: describes the eye as a flesh organ.

387
00:34:09,476 --> 00:34:10,476
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is a big difference.

388
00:34:11,556 --> 00:34:14,697
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha described that and described it as a present phenomenon.

389
00:34:15,197 --> 00:34:16,397
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the only way you can describe it.

390
00:34:17,518 --> 00:34:19,698
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly see your eye directly with your eye.

391
00:34:20,678 --> 00:34:23,559
[SPEAKER_00]: You can infer its presence through other senses.

392
00:34:25,139 --> 00:34:31,461
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the same thing the scientists would do just in regard to other people, senses.

393
00:34:33,452 --> 00:34:49,327
[SPEAKER_00]: But the difference is, for Buddhist practice, that inference, when you when you infer your eye as the reason for your sight, you include simultaneously the present sight in that inference.

394
00:34:51,983 --> 00:34:55,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, the whole thing, the whole inference happens on the basis of mindfulness.

395
00:34:56,645 --> 00:35:04,549
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you start discerning the simultaneous principle of the determination and that which is being determined, that simultaneously standing upon it.

396
00:35:05,309 --> 00:35:06,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Scientist doesn't do that.

397
00:35:06,710 --> 00:35:13,833
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't do that because his assumptions of himself, of his world, are already far out external in the world.

398
00:35:14,673 --> 00:35:29,087
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even when he looks at the other people's eyes, even if he says, my eyes are like the same, he... that doesn't pertain anywhere nearly to that which his eyes actually are in that experience, as that determination upon which the whole world of his stands.

399
00:35:40,843 --> 00:35:46,594
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by... by... again, be mindful of the... the sense bases.

400
00:35:52,104 --> 00:35:52,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Then,

401
00:35:55,851 --> 00:36:01,452
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, as I said, that is the closest you can see them.

402
00:36:01,792 --> 00:36:06,713
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the direct way of seeing them, as these third-party objects that are out there in the world.

403
00:36:07,093 --> 00:36:09,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But everybody can do that.

404
00:36:09,614 --> 00:36:16,875
[SPEAKER_00]: But not everybody can basically see them as simultaneously as determinations of that which you're seeing, i.e.

405
00:36:16,955 --> 00:36:17,155
[SPEAKER_00]: them.

406
00:36:21,236 --> 00:36:22,796
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can be thinking about your eyes,

407
00:36:24,347 --> 00:36:28,289
[SPEAKER_00]: Or think about that just vision as a domain.

408
00:36:29,709 --> 00:36:50,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see those, all of those thoughts, all of the thoughts that have resulted on account of it, all of the thoughts that will result in the future, on account of anything seen, that whole sphere, that whole domain, is right there and then, simultaneously determined by the still working presence of the fleshy eyes in your head, as you are thinking those very thoughts.

409
00:36:51,914 --> 00:37:04,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what no scientist can do, because if they were able to do that, they would basically fall back, or rather they would abandon the scientific view, which is the view of the external realities, and so on.

410
00:37:10,831 --> 00:37:25,182
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, if you see that simultaneously, and if you cultivate that simultaneous principle, then no matter what else simultaneously arises, no matter how inciting, how frightening the sight might be, it won't actually neither incite you, nor frighten you.

411
00:37:25,962 --> 00:37:26,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

412
00:37:26,203 --> 00:37:39,733
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the mindfulness remains unwavering, properly established in regard to the presence of the fleshly organs that are the simultaneous cause, determination, reason for the sight in the first place, whatever particularly that sight might be.

413
00:37:52,461 --> 00:38:03,149
[SPEAKER_00]: It's funny enough to... it's not appropriate, you know... No, because what you're not appropriating then, is your thought of what you think the I is,

414
00:38:07,332 --> 00:38:12,914
[SPEAKER_00]: That's then not being seen as simultaneously determined by the presence of the fleshy eye in your head.

415
00:38:14,014 --> 00:38:23,478
[SPEAKER_00]: But if your mindfulness starts revolving around the presence of the fleshy eye in your head, that means your mindfulness becomes established upon that which is

416
00:38:24,588 --> 00:38:26,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is going to be there for as long as you live, basically.

417
00:38:27,650 --> 00:38:32,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is another way of just saying developing mindfulness of the body to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

418
00:38:32,753 --> 00:38:36,795
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's eye, nose, ear, or basically sense basis, it's the body.

419
00:38:36,855 --> 00:38:45,620
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm so mindful of the whole body... Again, you can do that wrongly in the same sense you just described, that you can be wrongly mindful of your eyes.

420
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:46,821
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I'm mindful of the body.

421
00:38:46,881 --> 00:38:48,222
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm observing the body.

422
00:38:49,643 --> 00:38:53,545
[SPEAKER_00]: But that leaves the observer, or rather the assumption of the observer, out.

423
00:38:55,045 --> 00:39:12,359
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't observe the body, if you find the body is already there, because of which the entire actions that you're doing right now, aimed at this body, such as mindfulness of the body or whatever else, depend upon the presence of the body in the first place, then there is no room for that observer to step outside of it.

424
00:39:12,699 --> 00:39:13,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything is included.

425
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:22,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Because everything becomes undermined by your recognition that it all depends upon the external presence of this physical body, which I have no control over.

426
00:39:29,661 --> 00:39:35,184
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to be doing mindfulness.

427
00:39:35,204 --> 00:39:39,205
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to develop to the point where mindfulness does you.

428
00:39:56,365 --> 00:40:00,353
[SPEAKER_00]: Where your central self becomes secondary in regard to the mind.

429
00:40:42,772 --> 00:40:44,494
[SPEAKER_01]: It's secluded from unwholesome thoughts.

430
00:40:45,715 --> 00:40:48,898
[SPEAKER_01]: It's secluded from sensual pleasures, and it's secluded from unwholesome thoughts.

431
00:40:53,622 --> 00:40:55,423
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not appropriate on a sense basis.

432
00:40:56,885 --> 00:40:57,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It's secluded from the body.

433
00:41:00,308 --> 00:41:01,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind from the body.

434
00:41:02,710 --> 00:41:07,895
[SPEAKER_01]: It is secluded, or separated, or detached from unwholesome thoughts.

435
00:41:11,415 --> 00:41:24,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, not detached, in a sense, but basically, the arising of unwholesome thoughts cannot be affecting you, cannot be appropriate, because you're mindful of the basis of their arising.

436
00:41:27,398 --> 00:41:29,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is in regard to the body.

437
00:41:30,038 --> 00:41:30,318
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

438
00:41:30,338 --> 00:41:40,000
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you disown the body, even temporarily, so to speak, nothing that comes on account of the body, including thoughts, can't be yours, can't be affecting.

439
00:41:41,115 --> 00:41:43,217
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what's being recognized in the first jhāna.

440
00:41:44,198 --> 00:41:45,959
[SPEAKER_00]: That's a pleasure of seclusion.

441
00:41:47,080 --> 00:41:52,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure, basically, of... and confidence of, oh, you know, that you're safe here.

442
00:41:52,845 --> 00:41:58,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing that arises on account of your experience as a whole can touch you.

443
00:41:58,410 --> 00:41:59,271
[SPEAKER_01]: What is safe?

444
00:41:59,972 --> 00:42:00,572
[SPEAKER_01]: What is safe?

445
00:42:00,772 --> 00:42:01,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Safe.

446
00:42:01,633 --> 00:42:01,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

447
00:42:01,853 --> 00:42:02,494
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of safety.

448
00:42:02,914 --> 00:42:05,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... Why does anything need to be safe?

449
00:42:05,737 --> 00:42:07,819
[SPEAKER_00]: You can just have sense of safety in regard to these things.

450
00:42:08,767 --> 00:42:10,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't say, I'm...

451
00:42:10,629 --> 00:42:11,129
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm safe.

452
00:42:11,530 --> 00:42:19,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can say, I'm safe, but they won't stand for the sense of I am, in terms of appropriating the sense basis, because through not appropriating the sense basis, you become safe.

453
00:42:21,139 --> 00:42:23,822
[SPEAKER_00]: But sure, if you want to designate it, you have to say it like that.

454
00:42:23,902 --> 00:42:29,207
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other way.

455
00:42:29,327 --> 00:42:30,448
[SPEAKER_01]: The sense of safety is...

456
00:42:32,185 --> 00:42:33,745
[SPEAKER_01]: I go inside, close the door.

457
00:42:34,206 --> 00:42:34,746
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm safe.

458
00:42:35,426 --> 00:42:44,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, you can extrapolate the nature of feeling safe and just take it up to the ultimate level, where you feel safe from your own senses.

459
00:42:47,029 --> 00:42:50,450
[SPEAKER_00]: When you feel safe from the sights, you feel safe from thoughts.

460
00:42:52,411 --> 00:42:53,911
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't mean they still don't arise.

461
00:42:55,212 --> 00:42:56,132
[SPEAKER_00]: They very much arise.

462
00:42:57,032 --> 00:42:59,753
[SPEAKER_00]: But they can't take you with them wherever they go.

463
00:43:03,920 --> 00:43:11,365
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't move in regard to the movement of thinking and pondering, or in regard to the movement that that thinking and pondering is.

464
00:43:21,112 --> 00:43:33,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is safety from, like, there is seclusion from those senses,

465
00:43:34,102 --> 00:43:35,163
[SPEAKER_01]: One line of awesome thoughts.

466
00:43:35,963 --> 00:43:40,025
[SPEAKER_01]: There's safety, there's joy from seclusion.

467
00:43:40,065 --> 00:43:40,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

468
00:43:44,387 --> 00:43:49,230
[SPEAKER_01]: But, to say it, I am... Well, as I said, you can say it like that.

469
00:43:49,610 --> 00:43:56,133
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, as an experience, it's not... you don't feel that I am happy.

470
00:43:56,814 --> 00:43:57,314
[SPEAKER_01]: I am happy.

471
00:43:57,434 --> 00:43:57,854
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

472
00:43:58,014 --> 00:43:58,775
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't feel that I am.

473
00:43:58,795 --> 00:43:59,775
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't.

474
00:44:00,015 --> 00:44:01,136
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't possibly feel that.

475
00:44:01,996 --> 00:44:03,277
[SPEAKER_00]: Because otherwise there wouldn't be a jhāna.

476
00:44:29,403 --> 00:44:34,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Even any any quality that comes about from those

477
00:44:35,535 --> 00:44:41,479
[SPEAKER_01]: From being in the jhāna, instead of developing mind, any quality that comes about is not appropriate.

478
00:44:41,859 --> 00:44:42,199
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

479
00:44:42,219 --> 00:44:44,221
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why jhāna is wholesome in itself.

480
00:44:47,563 --> 00:44:51,165
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is why another jhāna is not an oblivious state.

481
00:44:51,905 --> 00:44:55,027
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a state fully founded upon unwavering mindfulness.

482
00:44:57,689 --> 00:44:58,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is inappropriate.

483
00:44:59,410 --> 00:45:00,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah.

484
00:45:00,211 --> 00:45:05,094
[SPEAKER_00]: If the mindfulness is established to a sufficient extent necessary, procreation becomes impossible.

485
00:45:06,494 --> 00:45:12,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Because appropriation is the result of mistake, misconception, unmindfulness, non-mindful attitude.

486
00:45:13,376 --> 00:45:18,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Results in assumption, results in appropriation, results in maintenance of the ignorance.

487
00:45:18,818 --> 00:45:20,478
[SPEAKER_00]: Practicing the opposite undoes that.

488
00:45:21,759 --> 00:45:30,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can only... the only person that can be mindful, right mind, is the person who doesn't appropriate the mind.

489
00:45:30,481 --> 00:45:35,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, the person who knows what mindfulness is, has knowledge of mindfulness.

490
00:45:39,407 --> 00:45:47,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is not just a pure, indifferent state, technique, method that you engage in.

491
00:45:47,594 --> 00:45:52,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

492
00:45:52,077 --> 00:45:59,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't do jhāna and then get to a state where you don't appropriate things anymore.

493
00:45:59,963 --> 00:46:00,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

494
00:46:00,404 --> 00:46:00,984
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't follow that.

495
00:46:01,244 --> 00:46:06,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, practice jhāna, practice mindfulness of breathing... To enter the state of...

496
00:46:07,485 --> 00:46:07,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

497
00:46:07,886 --> 00:46:08,486
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no, no.

498
00:46:08,506 --> 00:46:12,690
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to enter the jhāna, you already need to disappropriate.

499
00:46:12,971 --> 00:46:13,211
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

500
00:46:15,233 --> 00:46:22,700
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to... in order for mind to get settled in jhāna, the mindfulness needs to have been practiced.

501
00:46:23,861 --> 00:46:25,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, quite a lot.

502
00:46:27,645 --> 00:46:30,007
[SPEAKER_00]: And develop such a wide basis of mindfulness,

503
00:46:30,993 --> 00:46:34,016
[SPEAKER_00]: whereby it gets established regardless of what's happening around you.

504
00:46:35,017 --> 00:46:40,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, on certain occasions, you can then just dwell on certain aspects of that establishment and enter the jhāna.

505
00:46:41,290 --> 00:46:48,214
[SPEAKER_00]: And certain aspects of the establishments is the aspect of seclusion, aspect of safety, aspect of confidence, aspect of thinking and pondering.

506
00:46:48,234 --> 00:46:50,155
[SPEAKER_01]: That's directing the mind to those aspects.

507
00:46:50,235 --> 00:46:50,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

508
00:46:50,976 --> 00:46:55,878
[SPEAKER_01]: So, someone tells you that those aspects are found in such a state.

509
00:46:56,019 --> 00:46:56,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

510
00:46:56,459 --> 00:47:10,327
[SPEAKER_00]: If your basis of mindfulness is developed to such a wide extent, that already contains all these things, and you already see them clearly, then all you need to do is hear what the first jhāna is, and by thinking about it, you enter it.

511
00:47:11,068 --> 00:47:12,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is exactly what happened to Maha Moggallāna.

512
00:47:13,690 --> 00:47:16,953
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha told him, you should practice the first jhāna and study your mind in the first jhāna.

513
00:47:17,793 --> 00:47:18,894
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he thought, okay.

514
00:47:19,034 --> 00:47:25,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he went to the forest, sat under the tree, or on a rock, I can't remember, and said, first jhāna, first jhāna.

515
00:47:25,099 --> 00:47:25,980
[SPEAKER_00]: What is first jhāna?

516
00:47:26,220 --> 00:47:28,002
[SPEAKER_00]: What did the Lord mean by saying first jhāna?

517
00:47:28,062 --> 00:47:32,065
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, he just recollected all the things that the Buddha said the first jhāna is.

518
00:47:33,653 --> 00:47:35,734
[SPEAKER_00]: And by recollecting them, he entered the first jhāna.

519
00:47:35,914 --> 00:47:36,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

520
00:47:36,194 --> 00:47:41,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Because his mindfulness was already developed to such an extent that he could discern these things.

521
00:47:42,076 --> 00:47:44,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Most people can't, because most people's mindfulness is not established.

522
00:47:44,897 --> 00:47:55,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And it never will be, as long as their practice of meditation, in inverted commas, revolves around methods and techniques of observance, that somehow leads you into a state of, most often, oblivion.

523
00:48:01,222 --> 00:48:01,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

524
00:48:04,650 --> 00:48:07,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Just like, for example, mindfulness of the body.

525
00:48:07,931 --> 00:48:29,001
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, here a bhikkhu gone to the forest or the root of a tree, or an empty hut, sits down, and then folded his legs crosswise, sets his body erect, and establishes mindfulness in front of him, or establishes mindfulness, parimal kāma, around his mouth or around his face.

526
00:48:32,013 --> 00:48:34,754
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Pārimukha means, literally, around the mouth.

527
00:48:35,894 --> 00:48:36,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Around the mouth.

528
00:48:37,495 --> 00:48:45,597
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's already... he establishes mindfulness correctly.

529
00:48:45,637 --> 00:48:50,679
[SPEAKER_00]: Say, even if you dispute that, if you say it doesn't mean around the mouth, it means to the front of the face, basically.

530
00:48:50,699 --> 00:48:50,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

531
00:48:51,399 --> 00:48:51,939
[SPEAKER_00]: It means that.

532
00:48:52,059 --> 00:48:59,942
[SPEAKER_00]: So, by establishing your mind, your mindfulness, in regard to the front of the collection of your sense bases,

533
00:49:00,887 --> 00:49:01,848
[SPEAKER_00]: In front of yourself.

534
00:49:02,528 --> 00:49:02,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Pretty much.

535
00:49:03,208 --> 00:49:04,749
[SPEAKER_00]: In front of yourself.

536
00:49:05,289 --> 00:49:14,054
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, I'm discerning the reasons that underlie your sense of self, as an external given things.

537
00:49:15,435 --> 00:49:28,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, oh, if I can't own the reasons of my sense of self, if I can't own and control that which determines my very sense of self, which is this sense, which says, I can't possibly have the sense of self as mine either.

538
00:49:29,427 --> 00:49:30,448
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what anatta is.

539
00:49:30,528 --> 00:49:33,109
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not self.

540
00:49:33,970 --> 00:49:35,271
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not no self.

541
00:49:36,332 --> 00:49:43,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that which you've taken to be a self, it still stands there, but now it's seen as something that cannot be yours.

542
00:49:43,557 --> 00:49:45,258
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it's not self anymore.

543
00:49:45,278 --> 00:49:46,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Not myself.

544
00:49:47,779 --> 00:49:48,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible to own.

545
00:49:51,702 --> 00:49:53,683
[SPEAKER_01]: In the first section, it's necessary...

546
00:49:54,124 --> 00:49:55,825
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he establishes mindfulness.

547
00:49:56,325 --> 00:49:57,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness in front of him.

548
00:49:59,470 --> 00:50:01,333
[SPEAKER_01]: Ever mindful, you breathe in.

549
00:50:02,314 --> 00:50:04,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Ever mindful, you breathe out.

550
00:50:05,257 --> 00:50:18,433
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by having that established mindfulness, properly in front of yourself, I'll just say this, in front of your faith, in front of your appropriation,

551
00:50:22,462 --> 00:50:23,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, let the body do its thing.

552
00:50:24,483 --> 00:50:25,924
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're noticing now.

553
00:50:25,944 --> 00:50:27,165
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you got that established.

554
00:50:27,185 --> 00:50:28,425
[SPEAKER_01]: But from a different direction.

555
00:50:29,046 --> 00:50:29,966
[SPEAKER_01]: You notice now this.

556
00:50:30,046 --> 00:50:30,727
[SPEAKER_01]: There's the breath.

557
00:50:37,591 --> 00:50:38,851
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you do mindfulness.

558
00:50:38,891 --> 00:50:39,892
[SPEAKER_01]: That's mindfulness.

559
00:50:40,092 --> 00:50:40,772
[SPEAKER_00]: It's noticing.

560
00:50:41,553 --> 00:50:50,338
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can also notice, when you're anchored in that mindfulness, you can also then notice the sense of I am behind that breath.

561
00:50:51,372 --> 00:50:55,716
[SPEAKER_00]: But now you're noticing it from that external anchor position in front.

562
00:50:58,958 --> 00:50:59,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

563
00:50:59,679 --> 00:51:02,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that self is... is... is... is quite everywhere.

564
00:51:02,782 --> 00:51:06,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, quite everywhere, but now you are not there with it.

565
00:51:07,811 --> 00:51:11,652
[SPEAKER_00]: You're with that mindfulness to the fore, mindfulness around the mouth, mindfulness in the front.

566
00:51:12,432 --> 00:51:14,413
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that becomes now your viewing point.

567
00:51:14,653 --> 00:51:16,174
[SPEAKER_00]: There has no room for the self in it.

568
00:51:16,694 --> 00:51:27,997
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you see that... you see that body breathing, you see in-breath, out-breath, whatever, you see the act of breathing, and then you even see on top of the act of breathing, in the further distance, you see that sense of self.

569
00:51:28,758 --> 00:51:36,060
[SPEAKER_00]: But seeing it from a different angle means, oh, this is just another phenomenon that arises in regard to things, as pretty much all the other phenomenons.

570
00:51:37,965 --> 00:51:43,107
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, it shares the nature of all the other phenomenons, which is, they are not self.

571
00:51:45,267 --> 00:51:54,670
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it becomes apparent that you had your sense of self, not because there was self, but because you had the attitude of regarding things as mine.

572
00:51:56,010 --> 00:52:00,052
[SPEAKER_00]: People think I am, thus things are mine.

573
00:52:00,712 --> 00:52:01,532
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually the other way around.

574
00:52:01,652 --> 00:52:05,173
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you regard things as mine, you generate the sense of I am.

575
00:52:13,778 --> 00:52:29,024
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing the self as not-self makes that which used to be taken as self now just remain a selfless individual.

576
00:52:30,504 --> 00:52:39,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Individual in terms of still like a reflection of the five aggregates.

577
00:52:40,893 --> 00:52:53,096
[SPEAKER_00]: So to speak, the experience as a whole has its own, basically... Well, let's say, it has like a meeting point, which would be the mind.

578
00:52:56,797 --> 00:52:59,357
[SPEAKER_00]: Like the sign of the mind that we spoke before as well.

579
00:53:01,058 --> 00:53:06,659
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a meeting point, which is where usually the... well, no, always, where the sense of self for Pūrṭhujana is.

580
00:53:08,085 --> 00:53:09,506
[SPEAKER_00]: It's where the sign of the mind is.

581
00:53:10,247 --> 00:53:16,612
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, that's why it's rather easy to keep maintaining this, because most people don't even see that sign of the mind.

582
00:53:17,013 --> 00:53:21,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And seeing sign of the mind just means seeing mind as a phenomenon, however general.

583
00:53:23,338 --> 00:53:30,885
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you start seeing the sign of the mind, you won't be able to establish mindfulness of any kind that's relevant.

584
00:53:31,724 --> 00:53:43,446
[SPEAKER_00]: So, like, for example, in satipaṭṭhāna sutta, mindful of the mind states, all that requires proper seeing of the sign of the mind, so you can discern it for what it is at the time.

585
00:53:44,727 --> 00:53:57,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's that very sign of the mind that is taken... it's because it just feels like a center of experience, it's taken to be that sense of self, that sense of I am.

586
00:53:59,697 --> 00:54:15,248
[SPEAKER_00]: But if then you start developing, through the mindfulness properly established, pretty much like an external point of view, towards your own internal situation, then that sign of mind, in that sense of self, just become the extensions of the same principle of arising phenomena.

587
00:54:18,070 --> 00:54:18,790
[SPEAKER_00]: In what I just said.

588
00:54:19,751 --> 00:54:21,993
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if your mindfulness is established in front,

589
00:54:23,730 --> 00:54:24,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, there is an active breathing.

590
00:54:25,411 --> 00:54:29,133
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, while breathing, through that active breathing, you discern the sense of I am.

591
00:54:30,274 --> 00:54:31,755
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mind doesn't go back to the sense of I am.

592
00:54:31,835 --> 00:54:32,856
[SPEAKER_00]: It stays in the front.

593
00:54:33,816 --> 00:54:36,638
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, it looks back and it sees that sense of I am still there.

594
00:54:38,059 --> 00:54:44,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, it's another indifferent, independent phenomenon that has arisen, same like all the other phenomena.

595
00:54:45,327 --> 00:54:46,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means it's not me.

596
00:54:46,568 --> 00:54:47,108
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not mine.

597
00:54:47,188 --> 00:54:47,889
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not I am.

598
00:54:48,029 --> 00:54:50,991
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that special center that was always behind things.

599
00:54:51,031 --> 00:54:52,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Because now, look, I just see it as one of the things.

600
00:54:53,013 --> 00:54:53,533
[SPEAKER_00]: It's out there.

601
00:54:54,814 --> 00:54:57,356
[SPEAKER_00]: And if these things disappear, it cannot remain standing.

602
00:55:01,259 --> 00:55:15,049
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you want to look closer at that sense of I am, most often you find like a basically being underlined by the notions and concepts and images involving your own face, your own physical appearance.

603
00:55:17,922 --> 00:55:20,744
[SPEAKER_00]: Your own point of view of the appropriated eyes.

604
00:55:23,406 --> 00:55:24,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, inside.

605
00:55:25,947 --> 00:55:36,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But now that here, inside, is seen as just external... another... external thing... external thing arisen upon external determinations.

606
00:55:38,435 --> 00:55:44,619
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, no matter how internal it is, it cannot be internally taken as mine anymore.

607
00:56:02,491 --> 00:56:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you don't get rid of it.

608
00:56:06,473 --> 00:56:07,934
[SPEAKER_00]: You just stop regarding it as yours.

609
00:56:09,035 --> 00:56:10,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it ceases to be your subjectivity.

610
00:56:11,296 --> 00:56:12,697
[SPEAKER_00]: It just becomes your individuality.

611
00:56:22,516 --> 00:56:32,398
[SPEAKER_00]: And you regard... you stop regarding it as yours, not by saying, this is not mine, because that becomes another part of your subjectivity, but by developing point of view that has no room for it.

612
00:56:33,999 --> 00:56:42,521
[SPEAKER_00]: And that point of view is the view of... in front, around the mouth, around the face, around the head, or ahead of the sense bases.

613
00:57:10,568 --> 00:57:16,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is much... yeah, well, to be mindful, you have to have a body.

614
00:57:17,092 --> 00:57:18,553
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you have to be alive.

615
00:57:19,334 --> 00:57:32,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, but... but mindfulness being much larger, you know, larger in a sense, it covers all.

616
00:57:33,903 --> 00:57:36,845
[SPEAKER_01]: It can cover all things.

617
00:57:37,605 --> 00:57:38,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It can be in front of everything.

618
00:57:40,428 --> 00:57:41,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it can't be in front of the body.

619
00:57:43,229 --> 00:57:43,669
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness?

620
00:57:43,969 --> 00:57:44,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

621
00:57:45,210 --> 00:57:58,454
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, sorry, it can be in front, in the sense of the practice, but it can't go underneath the body, can't go underneath the matter, which is why it's possible to be mindful in the first place.

622
00:58:02,095 --> 00:58:02,675
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't go underneath?

623
00:58:02,695 --> 00:58:04,576
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because the body is already there.

624
00:58:05,336 --> 00:58:07,037
[SPEAKER_00]: The sense bases are already there.

625
00:58:08,097 --> 00:58:18,907
[SPEAKER_00]: You can establish your mindfulness upon that, upon the physical presence of the sense organs, upon the physical presence of the body out there.

626
00:58:21,489 --> 00:58:23,591
[SPEAKER_00]: But that mindfulness can't step outside of that.

627
00:58:23,631 --> 00:58:25,973
[SPEAKER_00]: That would mean mindfulness stepping outside of the five aggregates.

628
00:58:27,674 --> 00:58:28,435
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's impossible.

629
00:58:37,020 --> 00:58:37,741
[SPEAKER_00]: But I know what you mean.

630
00:58:37,861 --> 00:58:47,367
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say that mindfulness includes everything, that's... in that mindfulness, that includes everything.

631
00:58:48,007 --> 00:58:54,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Within that, you go further and you find the body already there, within that mindfulness.

632
00:58:56,233 --> 00:59:01,877
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sure, from the point of view of Putrajana's world, that mindfulness includes everything.

633
00:59:03,767 --> 00:59:04,647
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't stop there.

634
00:59:04,887 --> 00:59:13,551
[SPEAKER_00]: You look at it further and then you see that that mindfulness is basically founded upon the presence of a physical body that you have no control over.

635
00:59:21,033 --> 00:59:22,654
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, you can't appropriate that mindfulness.

636
00:59:22,754 --> 00:59:24,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply no room for any subjectivity.

637
00:59:25,255 --> 00:59:25,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Impossible.

638
00:59:44,618 --> 00:59:49,581
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's good to keep drilling this, because on some days, all these things will make perfect sense.

639
00:59:50,662 --> 00:59:52,222
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything will feel like it's all in the place.

640
00:59:53,683 --> 00:59:58,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, two, three days later, the doubt sets in, and then you just... it doesn't seem to fit with anything.

641
01:00:00,347 --> 01:00:01,207
[SPEAKER_00]: That's exactly the point.

642
01:00:01,627 --> 01:00:04,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you keep drilling, until it falls in place,

643
01:00:05,749 --> 01:00:06,709
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it falls out of place.

644
01:00:07,150 --> 01:00:08,570
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep trying until it falls in place.

645
01:00:09,510 --> 01:00:10,871
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it'll stop falling out of place.

646
01:00:12,492 --> 01:00:14,412
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you widen the basis of your mindfulness.

647
01:00:14,432 --> 01:00:16,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You widen the basis of your discernment.

648
01:00:16,813 --> 01:00:19,354
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, the doubt cannot enter it from any angle.

649
01:00:20,955 --> 01:00:25,057
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the doubt does enter it, instead of feeling like, oh my goodness, what did I do wrong?

650
01:00:25,117 --> 01:00:25,357
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

651
01:00:25,937 --> 01:00:29,938
[SPEAKER_00]: You take that as basically having your work cut out for you.

652
01:00:29,958 --> 01:00:30,319
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, see?

653
01:00:30,479 --> 01:00:31,859
[SPEAKER_00]: The doubt can enter it from this angle.

654
01:00:32,239 --> 01:00:33,500
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the angle I have to work now.

655
01:00:35,199 --> 01:00:36,160
[SPEAKER_00]: So, to remove the dove.

656
01:00:54,514 --> 01:01:00,359
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of the jhānas again, but in particular with this, with sūta, eh...

657
01:01:03,381 --> 01:01:04,362
[SPEAKER_01]: It is with the...

658
01:01:08,963 --> 01:01:23,535
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't do it from his point of view of the sense of self.

659
01:01:23,575 --> 01:01:25,136
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't do it from his subjectivity.

660
01:01:25,316 --> 01:01:30,540
[SPEAKER_00]: He does it from the externally established mindfulness to the fall, to the front.

661
01:01:31,541 --> 01:01:34,083
[SPEAKER_00]: From that point, he pervades this body.

662
01:01:49,440 --> 01:01:50,140
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentionally.

663
01:01:50,740 --> 01:01:50,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

664
01:01:53,642 --> 01:02:05,971
[SPEAKER_00]: But even that intention won't be appropriated then, because his mind is established to the fore, around the mouth, around the converging point.

665
01:02:08,768 --> 01:02:18,470
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the mind is established around the converging point, looking back from the converging point, then it pervades and fuses and so on.

666
01:02:18,690 --> 01:02:22,291
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything that's on the other side of the converging point, which is the entire bodily domain.

667
01:02:25,031 --> 01:02:25,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And he senses self.

668
01:02:26,792 --> 01:02:29,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's not there at the time, but that's where he senses self arises.

669
01:02:59,514 --> 01:03:07,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, it speaks of the bodily parts to gain your mindful of the converging point.

670
01:03:08,023 --> 01:03:16,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, all of those bodily parts, all of them, I mean, as anything that could stand for basically the physical

671
01:03:19,308 --> 01:03:22,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Physical aspect of your body, which is your body, basically.

672
01:03:23,009 --> 01:03:26,350
[SPEAKER_00]: All or any of those parts can serve as a converging point, if taken up readily.

673
01:03:26,911 --> 01:03:33,093
[SPEAKER_00]: And saying, seen as a converging point, is nothing other than saying, seeing is that because of which your whole world is already there.

674
01:03:33,973 --> 01:03:36,914
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it as a necessary condition to the experience as a whole.

675
01:03:37,334 --> 01:03:40,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing it as a determination to your existence.

676
01:03:42,196 --> 01:03:42,916
[SPEAKER_00]: Or for your entire life.

677
01:03:44,182 --> 01:03:45,063
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything that comes with it.

678
01:03:45,323 --> 01:03:51,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotions, feelings, happiness, sadness, ideas, hopes, ambitions, everything.

679
01:03:51,827 --> 01:03:52,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Urine, feces.

680
01:03:53,267 --> 01:03:55,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Urine, feces, nails, hair.

681
01:03:55,949 --> 01:03:59,591
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so your whole life stands upon the presence of these things.

682
01:03:59,611 --> 01:04:03,974
[SPEAKER_00]: That's... that's nothing to rejoice over, nothing to feel passionate about.

683
01:04:04,674 --> 01:04:09,477
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary, it's something to recognize, oh, this will come to an abrupt end.

684
01:04:10,452 --> 01:04:10,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Always.

685
01:04:11,212 --> 01:04:12,193
[SPEAKER_00]: Death is always abrupt.

686
01:04:12,913 --> 01:04:14,214
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this will come to an abrupt end.

687
01:04:14,574 --> 01:04:32,843
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's actually quite displeasing, quite frightening, the fact that it depends upon these random external things that are composed in this body, random matter, random elements, they have absolutely no say over, no control over, can't even conceive it in the most

688
01:04:35,122 --> 01:04:39,145
[SPEAKER_00]: In the wildest of abstractions, I can't conceive how it would be to control these elements.

689
01:04:39,225 --> 01:04:40,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just inconceivable.

690
01:04:40,605 --> 01:04:42,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, my whole life depends upon it.

691
01:04:42,527 --> 01:04:42,867
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything.

692
01:04:44,388 --> 01:04:52,173
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's... any passion towards it, any desire towards it, any... any joy towards it, would disappear.

693
01:04:54,974 --> 01:04:59,597
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for somebody who is still very much inside of it, that seems very frightening.

694
01:04:59,617 --> 01:05:00,718
[SPEAKER_00]: That would seem very frightening.

695
01:05:00,878 --> 01:05:01,638
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, my goodness, so what?

696
01:05:01,658 --> 01:05:03,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Nibbāna is a joyless state.

697
01:05:03,660 --> 01:05:04,200
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary,

698
01:05:05,567 --> 01:05:18,834
[SPEAKER_00]: The joy... well, basically, say you have a joy on account of drinking a pleasant beverage that's full of poison, that you can't taste, smell, touch, see... nothing.

699
01:05:18,854 --> 01:05:19,274
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't know.

700
01:05:19,775 --> 01:05:22,616
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't access that poison through any senses, but you know it's there.

701
01:05:23,597 --> 01:05:25,058
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the most pleasant beverage.

702
01:05:25,078 --> 01:05:26,238
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the simile from the suttas.

703
01:05:27,479 --> 01:05:31,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you don't know the poison is there, you'd be enjoying that beverage, wouldn't you?

704
01:05:32,682 --> 01:05:35,043
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's just pleasing in every possible aspect.

705
01:05:35,823 --> 01:05:40,345
[SPEAKER_00]: And then somebody tells you, eh... there is poison in it, that will kill you.

706
01:05:41,345 --> 01:05:45,807
[SPEAKER_00]: So, would... by not drinking it then, you would give up the joy of drinking it.

707
01:05:46,587 --> 01:05:53,069
[SPEAKER_00]: The joy of the pleasant taste, the joy of the pleasant consistency or whatever smell, the joy of the pleasant drink.

708
01:05:53,169 --> 01:05:53,929
[SPEAKER_00]: You would give that up.

709
01:05:54,350 --> 01:06:00,972
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you would then replace it with a much greater joy of knowing that you are basically not going to be killed.

710
01:06:01,787 --> 01:06:04,429
[SPEAKER_00]: By enjoying that poison.

711
01:06:04,589 --> 01:06:06,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, my goodness, I saved my own life.

712
01:06:10,734 --> 01:06:22,523
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, by abandoning the joys of worldly sensual life, the joys that arise on the basis of sense pleasures, you abandon all the dangers with it.

713
01:06:22,844 --> 01:06:27,347
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is... that can result in quite a non-worldly joy.

714
01:06:28,048 --> 01:06:29,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Joy of freedom, joy of liberation.

715
01:06:32,214 --> 01:06:46,787
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the, the, the joy.

716
01:06:47,007 --> 01:06:47,848
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the joy.

717
01:07:19,878 --> 01:07:28,865
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, it says, when it's developed and cultivated mindfulness of the body, Māra cannot find opportunity and support within.

718
01:07:29,526 --> 01:07:29,766
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

719
01:07:31,007 --> 01:07:34,450
[SPEAKER_01]: As it's been the other days, that Māra is yourself.

720
01:07:34,971 --> 01:07:36,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

721
01:07:36,532 --> 01:07:37,292
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you.

722
01:07:38,393 --> 01:07:40,755
[SPEAKER_00]: So, very dear sense of self, that's where Māra is.

723
01:07:45,780 --> 01:07:47,261
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you remove yourself from it,

724
01:07:48,772 --> 01:07:53,719
[SPEAKER_00]: Mahārāja can't find any basis on it, any support in it.

725
01:07:54,380 --> 01:07:56,062
[SPEAKER_00]: He can't see it, basically.

726
01:08:15,367 --> 01:08:19,109
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm sorry, in the suttas, mindfulness is the wakeful one.

727
01:08:20,850 --> 01:08:22,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is the wakeful one.

728
01:08:23,111 --> 01:08:23,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's have a new take.

729
01:08:29,315 --> 01:08:30,435
